<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Keos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Ioulis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cycladic island state of four small cities nearest to Attica, home of the poets Simonides and Bacchylides.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Keos lies off the tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and had a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> town at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-irini-kea">Ayia Irini</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> settled it, and the island was organised as a tetrapolis of four cities, Ioulis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karthaia">Karthaia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koressia">Koressia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poiessa">Poiessa</a>, which sometimes acted together as one state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Keos was famous for its poets: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a> and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacchylides">Bacchylides</a>, both from Ioulis, wrote for patrons across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> records a Keian law said to require citizens over sixty to drink hemlock. The island exported ruddle, a red earth pigment, and paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Keos joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-athenian-league">Second Athenian League</a> but revolted in the 360s BCE and was subdued. In the 3rd century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koressia">Koressia</a> became a Ptolemaic naval base renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-marion">Arsinoe</a>. The island passed under Roman control by 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:41.79%;width:15.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ioulis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kea, Ioulis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=keos" aria-label="Keos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5288 825 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Keos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5608 1081L5591 1068M5608 1081L5632 1088"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5632 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5608 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5608 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=keos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

